  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as Black.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|NBF0|BBF0|QBF0|KBF0|BBF0|    |RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|    |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |PBF1|    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |PWF1|    |    |    |NBF1|    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |PWF1|    |PWF1|BWF1| 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |    |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|    |PWF0|    |PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|    |BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|    |NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d5-d4, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, N: e4-c3, N: e4-c5, N: e4-d2, N: e4-d6, N: e4-f2, N: e4-f6, N: e4-g3, N: e4-g5, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and it's my turn to move. The board shows that White has advanced a few pawns, with a pawn on e4 (PWF1) and another on g3 (PWF1), both having moved one square from their starting positions. Notably, there is a white knight on e4 (NBF1 – actually this must be a typo in interpretation; rechecking, NBF1 at e4 is a black knight!), which indicates that my knight has already moved and is currently exerting influence in the center. White’s king is still on e1 (KWF0), likely unmoved, and castling rights may still exist for both sides.\n\nA key tactical feature is the presence of a black pawn on d5 (PBF1), which just moved two squares last turn (implied by its position and lack of T flag — but wait, encoding says PBF1: not 2-step eligible, so d5 was played one square earlier). Wait — correction: d5 pawn is PBF1 → 2Step=F → did NOT move 2 squares, so it came from d6 or d7. Actually, it's on d5 and marked F → so it moved only one square previously (from d6). So no en passant trigger from that.\n\nBut importantly, White has a pawn on a4 (PWF1), which could threaten queenside expansion. Also, the knight on e4 (NBF1) is mine — excellent central outpost. It’s attacking c3, c5, d2, f2, f6, d6, g3, g5 — several tempting squares.\n\nHowever, potential threats include the white bishop on h3 (BWF1), which is developed and eyeing the kingside, and the open g-file near my king. My kingside is weakened due to missing f7/g7/h7 pawns being intact except f7 and g7 have options — but I haven’t castled yet! In fact, my king is still on e8 (KBF0), rooks on a8 and h8 are unmoved. But h8-rook just has option R: h8-g8.\n\nMy pieces are mostly undeveloped: queen on d8, bishop on c8, knights on b8 and e4. The knight on e4 is well placed. There is a pawn on d5, semi-supported. White has no immediate check threats.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: d5-d4 – pushes the pawn, gaining space and potentially supporting the knight on e4. Could challenge control of e4 if needed. However, it might isolate the pawn or make it a target.\n- N: e4-c5 – attacks b7 or d7? No, c5 is central, threatens to jump into weaknesses. But limited follow-up.\n- N: e4-f6 – aggressive! Attacks the king-side. If White hasn't castled, or even if they have, Nf6+ could be disruptive. But is f6 protected? Currently, no black piece defends it, but White doesn’t have immediate recapture unless g-pawn or bishop takes.\nWait — let's assess N: e4-f6:\n   - Knight moves from e4 to f6\n   - Checks the white king on e1? Is it along diagonal? No — e4 to f6 is L-shaped: +2 file, +2 rank → yes, valid knight move.\n   - Does it give check? King is on e1. f6 is f6, e1 — difference: file +1, rank +5 → not attacking e1 directly. f6 attacks: g4, e4, d5, d7, e8, g8. So not checking king.\n   - But f6 is near the kingside, supports future attacks, pressures g4, e4, etc.\n- N: e4-g5 – threatens Qh5+, or prepares Bxh2 if h-pawn is vulnerable.\n- N: e4-g3 – forks queen and bishop? Let's see: g3 square — occupied? No, current state shows g3 has PWF1 (white pawn on g3)? Wait — no:\n\nLooking back:\nRow 3: g3 has PWF1 → white pawn on g3.\n\nSo g3 is occupied by a white pawn. Therefore, **N: e4-g3** would mean capturing that pawn!\n\nYes! e4 to g3 is a knight move: file +2, rank -1 → valid L-shape.\n\nAnd g3 has PWF1 → white pawn → so **N: e4-g3** is a capture!\n\nThat gains material: removing a white pawn, and the knight becomes active.\n\nAdditionally, after Nxg3, if White recaptures with fxg3, then the f-file opens toward White’s king (still on e1), and we can consider ...Qh4+ ideas later.\n\nAlternatively, if Rh1 is pinned or overloaded, this could be very strong.\n\nOther moves:\n- Q: d8-d7 – develops queen, connects rooks, defends b7 or prepares ...Rae8. Solid, but passive.\n- B: c8-g4 – pins the knight on e4? Wait — no, our own knight is on e4! We don't want to pin ourselves. But Bc8-h3 might attack g2, but long diagonal.\nWait — B: c8-h3 is in the list: yes, possible.\nBut B: c8-g4 is also listed — that’s lighter square bishop going to g4 — which would be useful to pin a knight if on f3, but White has no piece on f3. White knight on e4? No, that’s ours. White has no minor on kingside yet.\n\nActually, White has a bishop on h3 — same color as g4 — so Bh3 and Bg4 would be opposite colors.\n\nBg4 might prepare doubling pressure on h3 or support central play.\n\nBut more promising is the **knight capture on g3**.\n\nLet’s double-check: **N: e4-g3** — valid move? Yes, from e4 to g3: Δfile=+2, Δrank=-1 → valid knight move. And g3 has PWF1 → enemy pawn → so it's a capture.\n\nWhite cannot immediately recapture unless with f-pawn (f2-f3 or f2xf3), but f2 has no pawn shown — row 2: f2 has PWF0 → yes, white pawn on f2.\n\nSo after Nxg3, White can play fxg3, opening the f-file.\n\nBut now our queen can go to h4+, delivering check!\n\nAfter Nxg3, fxg3, then Qd8-h4+ is devastating:\n- Qh4+ checks the king on e1\n- Only legal responses: Ke2 (exposes to Qxh3), or g3-g4 to block? But g3 is behind, g4 is empty — yes, g3-g4 blocks.\nBut Qh4+ forces either:\n- Kd2? Illegal probably — not adjacent.\nKing moves: from e1, can go to e2, d1, d2, f1, f2.\n\nSo after Qh4+, king can go to:\n- e2: then Qxh3 wins the bishop outright.\n- f1: then Qf6+ or Qf4 next?\n- f2: Qxh3 again?\n\nIn all cases, losing the bishop on h3 is likely.\n\nTherefore, **N: e4-g3** initiates a powerful sequence leading to material gain or severe disruption.\n\nAlternative: **N: e4-f6** — attacks nothing immediately, less forcing.\n\nOr **P: d5-d4** — clamps down center, but slower.\n\nBut **N: e4-g3** is active, aggressive, and tactically sound.\n\nReason: This move wins material by force through a discovered attack setup: after Nxg3 fxg3, Qh4+ wins the bishop on h3 or disrupts White’s king position. It capitalizes on the exposed placement of White’s bishop and weak back rank.\n\nAnswer: N: e4-g3"}
