fore playing Nf2?\n\nFor example, play ...Bc8-f5 first, then Nf2? But that’s two moves.\n\nNot possible.\n\nSo Nf2 loses material.\n\nThus, not playable.\n\nBack to **d5-d4** vs **Nc5**.\n\n**N: e4-c5** is listed in valid moves.\n\nFrom e4 to c5: e→c (-2), 4→5 (+1) → L-shape — yes.\n\nc5 is empty — no piece.\n\nSo **N: e4-c5** is legal.\n\nAnd after Nc5, the knight is very strong.\n\nIt attacks d3 (central), b3 (queenside), and can go to a4, b7, d7, e6, f4, etc.\n\nWhite will struggle to remove it.\n\nAlso, it restricts White’s development.\n\nWhereas d5-d4 is solid but less ambitious.\n\nGiven that I’m slightly behind in development, I need to maximize activity.\n\n**N: e4-c5** improves my best-placed piece to an even better square.\n\nTherefore, I choose that.\n\nEvaluation:\n- N: e4-c5 — relocates knight to a strong outpost, increases pressure on White’s position, hard to counter\n- d5-d4 — solid central expansion, but less immediate impact\n- e7-e5 — dynamic, but can be met with d4\n\nReason: The knight on e4 is well-placed, but c5 is an excellent outpost, increasing its influence and restricting White’s play. It cannot be easily challenged and creates long-term pressure.\n\nAnswer: N: e4-c5'}
