, especially if they don’t want to trade.\n- **N: c5-b3**: Strong tactical move! The knight forks the queen on e2 (QWF1) and possibly attacks other valuable pieces. Let’s check: Nb3 attacks Qe2 directly — yes! And since White’s queen is undefended, this wins material immediately.\n  - After Nc5-b3, White cannot save the queen unless another piece defends it — but looking at the board: Qe2 is defended only by... nobody? King on e1 is too far, pawns don't cover it. Bishop on g2 (BWF1) doesn't defend e2. So yes — Nb3 wins the queen!\n- Other moves like Nc5-d7 or Qd8-d6 are solid developing moves, but not as impactful.\n- P: e7-e5 is also playable, gaining space, but slower than winning a queen.\n\nWait — is Nb3 actually legal? Let's confirm:\n- Knight on c5 (black) to b3 — b3 is currently empty (from the board: b3 is blank), so yes.\n- No obstruction for knight move (L-shape: c5 → b3 is 1 left, 2 down — valid).\n- Does it leave my king in check? No — knight isn’t pinned.\n- So **N: c5-b3** wins the white queen on e2.\n\nThis is a decisive move.\n\nReason: N: c5-b3 wins a queen for a knight, which is a massive material gain (queen = 9 points, knight = 3). Even though I lose the knight, capturing the queen gives me overwhelming advantage. There is no compensation for White. This move maximizes winning probability instantly.\n\nAnswer: N: c5-b3"}
