 So no Black pawn could have moved two squares on the previous move — because Black didn’t just move.\n\nHence, **PBT1 on f5 cannot have been moved last turn** — contradiction.\n\nUnless the "T" flag is outdated or incorrectly assigned.\n\nBut we must trust the encoding.\n\nAlternative possibility: perhaps the move history allows for Black to have moved something else previously, and earlier Black played ...f7-f5, but then White responded, and now it\'s Black’s turn again — but then the en passant window is closed (only one turn open), so the "T" flag should be "F".\n\nEn passant is only legal immediately after the two-square advance.\n\nTherefore, if it is now Black’s turn, then the previous move was by White, so any pawn that advanced two squares two moves ago is no longer en passant capturable.\n\nThus, **a Black pawn marked T at f5 implies it advanced two squares on the previous (Black) move**, which means White hasn’t moved yet — contradiction.\n\nConclusion: **There is an inconsistency in the game state.**\n\nBut since we must proceed, likely the **PBT1** is a typo or mislabeling.\n\nLooking again:\n\nAt f5: PBT1 — Black pawn, moved two squares last move → only possible if it\'s White’s turn.\n\nBut it\'s stated to be Black’s turn.\n\nTherefore, the only resolution is that **PBT1 is mislabeled**, or the turn is wrong.\n\nBut we are told: "You are playing as Black" and "now is your turn".\n\nSo we must assume the board is correct *except* possibly the 2Step flags for pawns not involved in immediate en passant.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "T" refers to whether the pawn *ever* moved two squares, but that contradicts the definition.\n\nDefinition says: "2Step: T - Moved 2 squares last turn"\n\nSo it must be last turn.\n\nThus, if a Black pawn has T, it moved two squares on Black’s last move → now it\'s White’s turn.\n\nBut here it\'s Black’s turn → impossible.\n\nTherefore, **the only pawn with T should not exist for Black if it\'s Black’s turn**.\n\nBut there is 