o b2? b2 is adjacent — yes, but is it attacked? Qd2 attacks b2? d2 to b2 is two left — horizontal — yes, same rank — so b2 is attacked by queen.\n\nSo possible king moves:\n- a3: attacked by Qd2 → unsafe\n- b2: attacked by Qd2 → unsafe\n- b4: attacked by Qd2 → unsafe\n- c3: attacked by Qd2 → unsafe\n- a4: not attacked → safe\n- c4: not attacked → safe\n\nSo White king can go to a4 or c4.\n\nAfter Ka4, I can play Qd2-b2+! Now, queen attacks a4? b2 to a4? No — b2 to a4 is diagonal? b2→a3 — not a4. So no. But I can play Na5-c6 or Na5-b3? Nb3 would be check again.\n\nBut more importantly, after Nc6-a5+ forces king to a4 or c4, I gain tempo and improve position, while White king is scattered.\n\nMoreover, after the check, I maintain initiative.\n\nOther moves like Q moves or rook lifts are slower.\n\nTherefore, **N: c6-a5** is a strong check, forcing the white king into a worse position, gaining tempo, and improving my knight.\n\nReason: **N: c6-a5** delivers check, exploits the exposed white king on b3, forces White into a passive king move (likely a4 or c4), and gains a crucial tempo in development and attack. It is the most forcing and advantageous move available.\n\nAnswer: N: c6-a5"}
