g diagonal toward a3/h7.\n  - Rooks on open files.\n  - Pawns on b7, d4, e5, h7.\n\nBut most importantly: **Can we attack the king on a4 directly?**\n\nCheck if any piece can **deliver check** to a4:\n\n- Queen on d2: d2 to a5 is one move away — a5 attacks b4, but not a4 directly. From d2, queen moves: d2-a5 is possible (in valid moves list), but a5 doesn’t attack a4 diagonally or straight. Wait — a5 to a4 is vertical, so queen on a5 could go to a4 next move.\n\nBut even better: **Knight on c4** — can it go to b2? Yes, N: c4-b2 is in the list. But b2 is not a4.\n\nc4 to a5? Yes, N: c4-a5 is listed. a5 is adjacent to a4 — but knight on a5 doesn't attack a4 (knight moves L-shaped). From a5, knight would attack b7, c6, c4 — not a4.\n\nWait — what about **Queen on d2 to b2**? Q: d2-b2 is in the list. From b2, queen would be on b2 — which is one square up from a4? No: b2 is file b, rank 2; a4 is file a, rank 4. Not adjacent.\n\nBut **b2 is two squares left and two up from a4** — not helpful.\n\nWait — let's map coordinates:\n\n- a4: file a, rank 4\n- Queen on d2: d-file, 2nd rank\n\nd2 to a5: that's three left, three up — yes, diagonal? d2-c3-b4-a5 — yes, that's diagonal. So queen can go to a5.\n\nFrom a5, queen threatens a4 vertically — yes! a5 to a4 is straight down the a-file.\n\nSo **after Qd2-a5**, next move Qa5xa4+ wins the white king unless prevented.\n\nBut is the king already in check?\n\nNo — currently, no black piece attacks a4.\n\nBut **N: c4-b2** — b2 is close. b2 to a4 is two up, one left — that's an L-shape! Yes! **Knight on c4 to b2 attacks a4**.\n\nBecause:\n- From b2: knight moves 2 up, 1 left → a4? 2 up from b2 is b4, then 1 left is a4 — yes!\n- Or 2 left (to file z?) — no, 2 left from b2 is off board.\n\nStandard knight moves from b2:\n- a4 (up 2, left 1) ✅\n- c4 (up 2, right 1)\n- d3 (right 2, up 1)\n- d1 (right 2, down 1)\n\nSo **yes — Nc4-b2 attacks a4 directly**.\n\nAnd since White king is on a4, **this move delivers check**!\n\nIs it checkmate?\n\nLet’s see: **N: c4-b2+** — knight checks king on a4.\n\nCan the king escape?\n\nKing on a4 can go to:\n- a3 (down one)\n- a5 (up one)\n- b3 (diagonally down-right)\n- b4 (diagonally up-right)\n- b5 (right two, up one — knight style? no — king moves one step)\n\nKing moves one square in any direction.\n\nSo from a4:\n- a3 (rank 3)\n- a5 (rank 5)\n- b3 (b3)\n- b4 (b4)\n- b5? No — only one square.\n\nSo possible escapes: a3, a5, b3, b4, b5? No — b5 is two up from b3 — no. Only adjacent.\n\nSo: a3, a5, b3, b4.\n\nNow, are these squares attacked?\n\nWe need to see if **all escape squares are covered**.\n\nBut first — is **N: c4-b2** in the valid move list?\n\nYes: **N: c4-b2** is listed.\n\nNow, after **Nc4-b2+**, where can White king go?\n\n1. **a3**: Is it attacked?\n   - By whom? Black has:\n     - Queen on d2 — can she attack a3? d2 to a3 is 3 left, 1 down — is that diagonal? d2–c1 = diagonal, but d2–c3–b4–a5 = upward diagonal. Downward: d2–c1 is one step. a3 is three left, one down — not on same diagonal. File difference 3, rank difference 1 — not diagonal or straight. So queen doesn't attack a3.\n     - Bishop on f8: too far.\n     - Pawn on b3? No — black has pawn on b3? Wait: current board shows:\n       - b3: PBF1 — yes! **Black pawn on b3**.\n       - pawns on b3 attack **a4 and c4 diagonally** — but wait, black pawn on b3: since black, it moves **downward**? No — black pawns move from higher ranks to lower, so from b3 to b2 or b1.\n\nPawn captures diagonally forward — for black, forward is downward (toward rank 1).\n\nSo black pawn on b3 can capture on **a2 or c2** — not a4 (which is upward).\n\nSo **pawn on b3 does NOT attack a4 or a3**.\n\nThus, **a3 is not attacked by any black piece**.\n\nSo king can escape to a3.\n\nTherefore, **Nc4-b2+ is not checkmate**, just check.\n\nBut still, it **wins material** — because if king moves, we can consider follow-up.\n\nBut wait — is there a **discovered attack** or something?\n\nAlternatively, can we **deliver check